WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:24.880
Speaker 1: I want to address a series&nbsp;
of articles that people sent me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:32.480
each in its own way, sort of saying Trump could&nbsp;
really be going down for some crimes here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.080
OK, now I'm going to give you my conclusion&nbsp;
up front. The most likely scenario is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.440
stuff doesn't happen. But there have been&nbsp;
enough articles and enough people wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.840
to me that I want to tell you what's being&nbsp;
said and you've got to look at the language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.840
A Business Insider article. Donald&nbsp;
Trump could be charged with multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.280
election multiple crimes over his attempts to&nbsp;
overturn his loss in Georgia, a report says.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.280
The article saying the report by the Brookings&nbsp;
Institution, a think tank in Washington, D.C.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.480
analyzed publicly available evidence that showed&nbsp;
Trump and his allies attempted to pressure Georgia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.200
officials to change the lawful outcome of the&nbsp;
election. A key piece of evidence is the now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.480
infamous Trump call, where he wanted to find&nbsp;
eleven thousand seven hundred and eighty votes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.800
There's no way I lost Georgia. There's no&nbsp;
way we won by hundreds of thousands of votes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.120
Those assertions were, of course, false. And&nbsp;
the report added that Trump publicly pressured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:31.280
and contacted Republican officials to ask for&nbsp;
their help. We conduct we conclude that Trump's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:37.040
post-election conduct in Georgia leaves him&nbsp;
at substantial risk of possible state charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:43.040
predicated on multiple crimes. These charges&nbsp;
potentially include criminal solicitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.640
to commit fraud, election fraud, intentional&nbsp;
interference with performance of election duties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.360
conspiracy to commit election fraud,&nbsp;
criminal solicitation and state RICO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.560
violations, the report adds liability&nbsp;
could extend to allies like Rudy Giuliani.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:06.000
Another article from Vanity Fair says report Trump&nbsp;
may be hit with multiple criminal charges over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.680
his effort to overturn the election in Georgia.&nbsp;
This is by best someone who's done a lot of great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:18.720
coverage over the years. It references the exact&nbsp;
same report from the Brookings Institution and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:25.520
it mentions the request to find eleven thousand&nbsp;
seven hundred and eighty votes. It refers to Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:32.160
pressure that happened publicly and privately&nbsp;
against Republican officials. And it again says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:37.360
Trump could be hit with that same list of charges&nbsp;
based on the same Brookings Institution report.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:44.720
And then lastly from The Detroit News, Trump&nbsp;
faces substantial legal risk in Georgia case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.880
Think Tank Report says this is from that same&nbsp;
Brookings Institution report and it lists the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.800
exact same stuff. So basic media literacy, first&nbsp;
things first. All of these articles are really one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.840
story. The Brookings Institution's opinion is&nbsp;
Trump could face substantial legal problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:13.520
What needs to be clarified is if he were a normal&nbsp;
person. I continue to see no appetite in charging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.800
Trump for what he did in Georgia. Should he&nbsp;
be charged? Yeah. Yeah. Could he be charged?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:25.600
Absolutely. The ongoing question is less about&nbsp;
could or should it happen, but will it happen now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.480
You can look at Georgia and you can say, well,&nbsp;
on the one hand, Republicans control basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.600
everything in Georgia, but they don't control&nbsp;
the Senate seats. But that is not involved. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.760
as I hit my mike, sorry, that is not involved&nbsp;
here. We're talking about state charges here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.480
And Republicans in Georgia aren't going to hold&nbsp;
Donald Trump accountable for anything. He operates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:52.240
with impunity. It's infuriating, but it's just&nbsp;
not going to happen. The other side of it is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:57.280
you know, the truth is these elected officials&nbsp;
in Georgia don't actually have anything to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.280
with what is is happening. It's&nbsp;
really the Fulton County D.A.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.120
and the Fulton County D.A. has&nbsp;
experience with organized crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.760
and may not be beholden to the political&nbsp;
interests of state Republicans in Georgia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:17.360
I don't know the answer. My continued belief&nbsp;
is Trump's not going down. Allies might.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.720
Other people might. I don't see Trump going&nbsp;
down. If you disagree with me, let me know.

